    "The auction for her grandmother's locket would close in forty minutes. Maya’s legs ached from the market run, and her stomach rumbled. Her cousin called, \"Just made fresh soup, come over, you look worn out. There's a warm blanket waiting.\" Maya pictured the locket, its worn clasp. She thought of the stories her grandmother used to tell, tracing the same floral etching with her thumb. Instead of walking towards her cousin's warm kitchen, Maya turned sharply down the busy high street, pulling out her phone to bid, even knowing it meant another hour on her feet, entirely unfed. Success meant holding the cool metal in her palm once more.",
